course: biology
lesson: 22
title: Elephants: families, tusks, ears and memory
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An elephant herd is a family of grandmothers, mothers, aunts, sisters and babies, and it walks in a line behind the oldest female, the matriarch, because she has lived longest and remembers the most.
explain: Think of a family trip led by Nan, who has done the drive for fifty years: in a drought, the matriarch remembers a waterhole the herd hasn't visited for years and leads them all there.
explain: An African elephant's two huge ears are like radiators full of blood vessels, so flapping them cools the blood; spread wide with a head shake, the same ears mean «back off».
explain: Tusks are two very long front teeth made of ivory that grow all life long, and elephants are left- or right-tusked, just as you are left- or right-handed.
explain: Dumbo flies with his ears and cartoons show elephants as clumsy and forgetful; real ears never lift an elephant off the ground, but they do cool it, and real elephants remember friends and places for decades.
fact: An elephant herd is a family of females and their young, led by the oldest female, called the matriarch, and the females share in looking after the calves.
fact: Young males leave the family at around 10 to 15 years old and live alone or in loose groups of males.
fact: An elephant mother is pregnant for about 22 months, the longest of any land animal, and the calf weighs about 100 kilograms at birth.
fact: A newborn calf stands within about an hour so that it can drink milk and keep up with the herd.
fact: Tusks are very long front teeth made of ivory that keep growing all through the elephant's life, but a tusk pulled out at the root never grows back.
fact: Elephants use their tusks to dig for water and salt, strip bark, move things and defend themselves.
fact: Poachers kill elephants for their tusks, and selling ivory is banned in most countries; where poachers killed many big-tusked elephants, more elephants are now born without tusks, because tuskless ones survived to have young.
fact: Elephant ears are full of blood vessels, and flapping them cools the blood; African elephants have much bigger ears than Asian elephants.
fact: Spreading the ears wide and shaking the head is a warning to stay back, and a wild elephant can run about 25 kilometres an hour.
fact: Elephants remember routes to far-away water and food for many years, and herds with older matriarchs survive droughts better.
fact: Elephants recognise old friends after years apart and greet them noisily with rumbles, ear flapping and trunk touches.
fact: Elephants touch and smell the bones of dead elephants; scientists think they know these are their own kind, but nobody knows exactly what they feel.
fact: The elephant graveyard, a secret place where elephants go to die, is a myth.
fact: An Asian elephant named Happy touched a mark on her head after seeing it in a mirror, which suggests she knew the reflection was herself; great apes, dolphins and magpies have passed this mirror test too.
exercise: Who leads an elephant herd? | The oldest female, called the matriarch | The biggest male, called the king | The youngest calf, so it stays safe | A different elephant every day
exercise: Why does the herd follow the oldest female? | She remembers where water and food are and how to deal with danger | She is always the fastest runner | She has the longest and heaviest tusks in the herd, so the others obey | The others are afraid of her trunk
exercise: At about what age do young male elephants leave the family? | About 10 to 15 years | About 1 year | About 40 years | They never leave their mothers
exercise: How long is an elephant mother pregnant? | About 22 months | About 9 months, like people | Only about 3 months | About 5 whole years
exercise: Why must a newborn calf stand up within about an hour? | To drink milk and keep up with the herd | Because the ground is too hot | So the keepers can weigh it | To start digging with its tusks
exercise: What are tusks? | Very long front teeth made of ivory | Horns made of hard hair, like a rhino's | Bones that stick out of the cheeks | Claws that moved up to the face
exercise: Which is a real use of an elephant's tusks? | Digging for water and salt | Breathing while under deep water | Hearing low rumbling sounds far away | Storing fat for the cold winter
exercise: Why is ivory poaching a crime? | Poachers kill elephants to take their tusks | It makes the elephants' tusks grow far too long | It only scares elephants for a day | It is not a crime anywhere
exercise: Why does an African elephant flap its huge ears on a hot day? | Flapping cools the blood flowing through the ears | It is waving to other elephants | It is shaking off its tusks | It is trying to lift itself up and fly away like a big bird
exercise: Which elephants have the bigger ears? | African elephants | Asian elephants, from India | Baby elephants of both kinds | Both have exactly the same ears
exercise: An elephant spreads its ears wide and shakes its head at you. What does it mean? | It is warning you to stay back | It wants you to come over and play | It is about to fall fast asleep | It is cooling down after a swim
exercise: In a drought, which herds tend to find far-away water best? | Herds led by older matriarchs | Herds led by strong young males | Herds with the most young calves | Herds that stay in one place all year
exercise: Two elephants meet after years apart and rumble and flap their ears. What is happening? | They recognise each other and are greeting an old friend | They are strangers, and they are about to fight over the waterhole | They are asking the way to water | They are cold and shivering
exercise: Elephants gently touch the bones of a dead elephant. What do scientists say? | They seem to know it was an elephant, but nobody knows exactly what they feel | They are looking for something to eat among the old bones | They are building a nest out of bones | Elephants ignore bones completely
exercise: What did the elephant Happy do in the mirror test? | She touched a mark on her own head after seeing it in the mirror | She attacked the other elephant that she thought she saw in the mirror | She tried to walk through the mirror | She ignored the mirror completely
exercise: Which statement about elephant graveyards is true? | They are a myth; elephants have no secret place to die | Every herd has one secret graveyard | Only African elephants use secret graveyards | Graveyards are where tusks are buried
test: Dumbo flies with his ears innit. could a real elephant? | No, but its ears do work as coolers and warning signals | Yes, if the ears are big enough | Yes, but only baby elephants | No, real elephants have no ears at all, only two small holes in the head
test: Count them: a herd has five adult elephants and three calves. How many ears is that? | 16 | 8 | 12 | 32
test: Why might a herd led by a young matriarch do worse in a drought? | She has fewer memories of far-away water | She can't walk as fast | Young females have no trunks | The other elephants refuse to follow a young female
test: My uncle says elephants can grow a new tusk if one is cut off. Right? | No, a tusk cut off at the root never grows back | Yes, a new one grows in a month | Yes, but only on Asian elephants | No, because elephants don't have any tusks to cut
test: What would you expect if poachers kill mostly elephants with big tusks for many years? | More elephants born without tusks, because tuskless ones survived | Tusks would grow twice as fast | Elephants would start to grow new tusks on their legs and backs instead | Nothing would change at all
test: An elephant always uses its left tusk to dig, and that tusk is worn shorter. What is this like? | Being left-handed | Being colour-blind | Having a broken leg | Being a different species
test: get out of the way!! the elephant's got its ears spread and its head shaking | Yes, back away slowly to a safe distance | No, run straight past it | Wave your arms and go closer | Climb up onto its back and stroke it to calm it down
test: The saying is "an elephant never forgets". How true is it? | Partly true: they remember friends and places for decades | Totally true: they remember every single thing | Not true at all: they forget in minutes | It's about their tusks, not their memory
test: Your friend says the oldest elephant leads because she's the biggest. What's the better reason? | She has the most experience and remembers the most | She has the loudest trumpet | She can carry all of the calves on her back at once when they are tired | She is the only one who can see
test: A one-year-old calf is surrounded by its mother and two aunts. Why do the aunts help? | Females in the herd share in looking after the calves | They want to take the calf for themselves | The mother has gone off to live with the big males and left her calf behind | The calf is their teacher
test: Which animals besides elephants have passed the mirror test? | Great apes, dolphins and magpies | Cats, cows and goldfish | Only humans | Snakes, frogs and snails
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an elephant herd is led by its eldest female. | Her accumulated memory of resources and dangers | Her superior bodily mass | The length of her tusks | Her status as the youngest member of the entire herd
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the thermoregulatory function of the elephant's auricles? | Blood circulating through them is cooled when they are flapped | They store water for periods of drought | They generate heat by means of friction | They serve no purpose whatsoever other than that of the perception of sound
exam: It is given that tusks are composed of ivory. Determine which anatomical category they belong to. | Teeth | Horns | Claws | Cheekbones
exam: State the approximate duration of gestation in the elephant, being the longest among terrestrial animals. | Twenty-two months | Nine months | Three months | Five years
